// prelude/mem — heap memory and byte operations.
//
// Ownership (Rust Ch 4.1): `alloc` returns a fresh owner; `*int q = p`
// moves `p`; `free(p)` consumes (`p` invalid after; use-after-free and
// double-free are compile errors); `memcpy` borrows. `x.move()` clones
// any object explicitly (source stays valid; `mem.retainVal(o)` is the
// same operation as a free function).
//
//   alloc(n) -> ptr   request n bytes from the heap (mmap-backed, 16-byte aligned)
//   free(p)          release p (consumes; no-op in v1; the process reclaims it at exit)
//   memcpy(d, s, n)  copy n bytes from s to d (borrows)
//   strlen(s) -> int  byte length of a NUL-terminated string (borrows)
//   mem.retain(o) -> int  bump the refcount of a class object (o)
//   mem.release(o)       drop one reference (a plain refcount decrement)
//   mem.retainVal(o) -> int  null-guarded retain that returns o (the value
//                           stays usable in a typed slot of any class;
//                           the explicit clone for shared ownership)
//
// Pointers are 64-bit; a buffer from `alloc` is indexed as 8-byte elements:
//   buf[i]  loads/stores the int at byte offset i*8.
int main() {
    *byte s = "memory";
    println(strlen(s));
    *int buf = alloc(8);
    buf[0] = 7;
    println(buf[0]);
    free(buf);
    return 0;
}
